{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1491176-alternate-setting-first-kill","id":1491176,"title":"Alternate Setting - First Kill","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2022-08-24T03:44:55-07:00","id":1491176,"title":"<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/icons/icon3.png\" width=\"16\" height=\"16\" alt=\"\" /> Alternate Setting - First Kill","content":"So, there is an author named V. E. Schwab, who wrote several young adult novel series and a short story named &quot;First Kill&quot; that she subsequently turned into an 8 episodes Netflix series, that was canceled after that.<br /> <br /> There was not that much exposition in the series, but some bits here and there and I was wondering, if that makes a good setting for either a hunter game or any other WoD game really.<br /> <br /> So, there are some spoilers for the Netflix show ahead...<br /> <br /> <br /> Basically the series revolves around one family of 'Legacies', vampires from a special bloodline dating back to Lilith and Eden, who are daywalkers and are born (despite their supposed age, only three generations are seen). They have the ability to turn humans into regular vampires, seemingly. The exact mechanics aren't clear for the mother of the family is said to have turned her human plaything into a vampire (and had three Legacy kids through sex), but he is seemingly also a daywalker, as he his the DA of Savannah. The &quot;First Kill&quot;, after which the show is named, is seemingly required when a born Legacy vampire hits puberty and develops a bloodlust.<br /> The bite of a Legacy is said to induce a love-like state in the victim, that lasts for a while. Also one Legacy has a power of mind/memory-control that is unique and she is asked not to use it by the other Legacies, because it is not well understood. Longevity is implied for vampires.<br /> <br /> The other family is a group of hunters. They are humans, but with the ability to somehow sense supernatural beings. Like a spider-man spider-tingling. This ability is seemingly also inherited. They train a lot to fight monsters and are initiated by performing a &quot;First Kill&quot;.<br /> <br /> What got me more interested is that, akin to True Blood, the knowledge that monsters exist in the world is practically public knowledge, while the specifics are unknown.<br /> Hunters first discuss, that the were monsters in Savannah before, but that it is monster-free for several years now, but later (out of the blue) normal people also discuss this. Also, in a later episode there is a &quot;Monster Alarm&quot; at High School, similar to an &quot;active shooter&quot; alarm and students know what to do. After the second monster-related death that becomes public knowledge, the parents of the high school kids form an anti-monster neighbourhood watch (despite having no clues about monsters aside of pop culture).<br /> <br /> Apart from vampires, there are zombies (fast running), witches, golem-like beings and other monsters seen over course of the episode.<br /> <br /> The Hunter family is backed by a group called the Guild. They even refer to themselves as &quot;guild-sanctioned hunters&quot;. Still, the hunter family's identities are secret to the public and they have cover identities. The Guild also has mystics, putting up wards in the house of the hunters, and archivists, collecting monster lore. They don't seem to be government sanctioned, though. More like an independent group. A non-profit maybe? The Guild members do perform post-operation debriefs with the hunters and seemingly act as some form of administration and oversight for them.<br /> <br /> Still, the Guild condones the hunters to raid monster layers, even of monsters who are human-like (vampires) and kill them without trail. Whereas when given evidence that a person had been collecting trophies of several dozen missing person cases (their driver's licenses), she is arrested and put in jail. So it seems, monsters might have rights in the eyes of the state.<br /> <br /> All in all, I found this setting of semi-secrecy surrounding the supernatural an interesting idea. Sure, True Blood had it too, while in Vampire Diaries/Originals, Teen Wolf, Lost Girl and other shows only individual humans knew of the supernatural.<br /> <br /> What do you think?<br /> Did anyone read the other novels by the author that might or might not share the same universe?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1008,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1008-heinrich","user":"heinrich","date":". 08-24-2022, 06:49 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1008-heinrich","name":"heinrich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1008_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1802"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}